call_details.response:
The position has no immediate wins or blocks available on this turn. All diagonal 4-in-a-rows are already intercepted by opposing pieces, as are all verticals except those in columns 5 and 6 and the top of column 0. Consequently the only remaining winning resources are:

- o occupying (2,5) (which completes a horizontal 4 already containing three o’s);
- four consecutive o’s in column 6;
- x occupying all three remaining cells of column 0;
- four consecutive x’s in column 6;
- x occupying both (3,5) and (2,6) (the last two cells of an open diagonal).

Column 5 is therefore “hot”: the first player to occupy it hands the opponent an immediate win on the following move unless the diagonal condition above is already satisfied. Perfect play therefore never touches column 5 until every other legal cell has been filled.

The 11 cells that then remain (3 in column 0, 2 in column 1, 6 in column 6) will be exhausted after 11 moves. Because that number is odd, it will be x who is forced to open column 5. If at that moment (2,6) already belongs to o, x’s move does not complete the diagonal, o answers at (2,5) and wins.

o can force ownership of (2,6) (the fourth cell of column 6) by treating the remaining cells of columns 0/1 as tempo moves and answering in column 6 on the correct parity. Playing column 0 on the first move simultaneously realises that parity strategy and permanently occupies one cell of x’s only other threat, so x can never obtain three-in-a-column there. After the 11 side cells are filled, the forced sequence in column 5 therefore wins for o.

(The same forcing plan exists after a first move of 1 or 6, but occupying column 0 at once removes x’s most dangerous counter-play and is therefore simplest.)

